        "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity",
        "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$",
        "worked_solution": "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad  \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
